4673,0.02101260796189308,0.019589537754654884,0.011927435174584389,-0.007517235353589058,-0.015411839820444584,0.0009723177063278854,-0.0028333901427686214,-0.004829098470509052,0.006412100978195667,-0.009267814457416534,-0.013025721535086632,-0.019764503464102745,0.0010397454025223851,-0.030981779098510742,0.007386732380837202,0.004392811097204685,0.015046070329844952,0.01983802579343319,0.0338011272251606,-0.016371095553040504,Predictive Reranking (Trained LM-Dedicated Reranker),"Further optimizing document selection for InContext RALM beyond general-purpose or zero-shot methods, by training a reranker specifically to predict which document will best aid the LM in generating the upcoming text.","An InContext RALM system has retrieved a set of 'k' candidate documents. There is availability of training data from the target corpus, and the goal is to achieve maximal LM performance by selecting the most relevant document.","Train a dedicated reranker (e.g., a fine-tuned RoBERTa model) as a classifier. For each training example, given an LM prefix and a candidate document, the reranker learns to produce a scalar score resembling the document's relevance for the continuation of the prefix. This training uses the LM's own signal (e.g., p(y|d, prefix)) as a target for relevance. The reranker then selects the document with the highest predicted relevance score.","Significant additional gains in LM performance (lower perplexity) compared to zero-shot reranking, demonstrating the effectiveness of domain-specific training for document selection.","Maximizing document relevance for RALM, achieving state-of-the-art performance in knowledge-intensive LM tasks, fine-tuning document selection for specific domains.","Predictive Reranking trains a dedicated model to optimize document selection for InContext Retrieval-Augmented Language Models, specifically predicting which retrieved document will best aid the LM in generating upcoming text. This reranker learns by using the LM's own performance signal (e.g., probability of continuation given document) as a target, scoring candidate documents based on their predicted relevance. The approach yields significant additional gains in LM performance, demonstrating the effectiveness of domain-specific training for document selection."
