-0.01581301912665367,0.006484017241746187,0.022374723106622696,-0.005827581509947777,-0.004206645768135786,-0.011384344659745693,0.008775327354669571,-0.009741922840476036,0.010979638434946537,-0.0023978999815881252,-0.01745365746319294,-0.017244307324290276,-0.02739027328789234,0.01037650927901268,-0.01858501322567463,0.01383624691516161,0.006315151695162058,0.00819496437907219,0.010441910475492477,0.03996434435248375,-0.024846743792295456,In-Context Learning for Tool Use,"LLMs need to learn how to correctly invoke and utilize a diverse set of external tools, including understanding their API signatures, arguments, and appropriate contexts, often within the limited context window of a prompt.","When augmenting LLMs with new tools, explicit fine-tuning for every tool combination can be impractical. LLMs need to generalize tool usage from limited examples.","Provide 'tool-level demonstrations' (few-shot examples) within the LLM's prompt. These demonstrations illustrate the correct syntax for calling tools, how to pass arguments, and how to interpret observations, effectively serving as a concise tutorial for tool interaction.","Enables LLMs to comprehend and compose different tools for question answering and problem-solving, improving their ability to generate valid tool calls and arguments without extensive fine-tuning.","Initializing LLMs for new tool sets, adapting to dynamic tool environments, reducing the need for large-scale tool-specific training data.","In-Context Learning for Tool Use addresses the challenge of Large Language Models (LLMs) needing to correctly invoke and utilize diverse external tools, understanding their APIs and appropriate contexts, often within limited prompt windows. The solution involves providing ""tool-level demonstrations"" as few-shot examples directly within the LLM's prompt, illustrating the correct syntax for calling tools, passing arguments, and interpreting observations. This method enables LLMs to effectively comprehend and compose different tools for problem-solving, improving their ability to generate valid tool calls and arguments without extensive fine-tuning."
