968494445085526,0.010703971609473228,0.013666348531842232,-0.01776636578142643,0.006814234424382448,-0.0017538840183988214,0.006133113522082567,0.00964586716145277,-0.0008803348755463958,-0.025712013244628906,-0.02414669282734394,0.01135782990604639,-0.03279072418808937,0.005977594293653965,-0.015594549477100372,-0.006389916408807039,0.01518489420413971,0.030132925137877464,-0.0241070669144392,Fixed Document Index (during Fine-tuning),"Updating the document encoder and rebuilding the entire document index during fine-tuning of a retrieval-augmented model is computationally costly and time-consuming, especially for large document corpora.","Fine-tuning a retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) model where the document corpus is large, and the document encoder is part of the end-to-end training loop.","Keep the document encoder (e.g., BERT_d) and the associated document index fixed during the fine-tuning phase. Only the query encoder (e.g., BERT_q) and the generator (e.g., BART) are fine-tuned. The document index is built once before fine-tuning.","Reduces computational cost and training time significantly, making fine-tuning more practical, while still achieving strong performance. This strategy is found to be 'not necessary for strong performance' in this context.","Optimizing the fine-tuning process for RAG models, reducing computational overhead.","This pattern, ""Fixed Document Index (during Fine-tuning),"" addresses the high computational cost of updating document encoders and rebuilding large document indices during the fine-tuning of retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) models. It solves this by keeping the document encoder and its index static, only fine-tuning the query encoder and generator. This approach significantly reduces training time and computational overhead, making the fine-tuning process more efficient and practical without sacrificing strong performance."
